  "asserts": "One `dbg` capture, two commands, one answer -- amendment A11 in the second dialect. `'A1'` is how `util.inspect` spells the string A1, so `watch --expr s == 'A1'` is SATISFIED at the site that holds it and `flow --value \"'A1'\"` sights the same capture: the reader that parses the text and the writer that spells the literal are inverses over the domain a command accepts, or one instrument says yes and the other no about one recorded value. Read as Rust's `Debug` -- the reader's only dialect before this rung -- that text spells the four-character word `'A1'` instead, so both answers would be wrong in the same silent way. Which dialect is used is the TRACE's own (`vocab.dbg_dialect`), never a guess from the text -- and the quotes are the spelling, not the value: `s == \"'A1'\"`, which compares against the four characters the other dialect would have read, is a decided no.",
